# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
# Any build dir BW_DIST_DIR points at (see next.config.ts), not just the
# documented `.next-verify`. Two reasons, and the second is the expensive one:
#   1. A build dir git can see is thousands of files in `git status`, and two
#      sessions share this repo and stage by name.
#   2. ⚠ TAILWIND v4 SCANS THE REPO FOR CLASS NAMES AND SKIPS WHAT GIT IGNORES,
#      so an un-ignored build dir becomes SOURCE. On 2026-10-28 a dev server on
#      `.next-dev` made Tailwind read its own compiled output back and emit
#      `.h-\[2\.7\!\3 R\] { height: 2.7!R }` into globals.css — every page
#      500ing with "Parsing CSS source code failed / Unexpected token
#      Delim('!')", pointing at a line in a file nobody had edited. The glob is
#      wide so the next person's suffix is covered too.
/.next-*/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# offline basemap assets (fetched by scripts/setup-map-data.sh; deployed via rsync working tree)
/public/basemaps/

# Supabase CLI scratch state — machine-specific, and actively misleading once
# the DB moves. These files still pointed at the retired hosted project long
# after the platform moved to self-hosted Supabase on the Studio, which is how
# `supabase db push --linked` came to target the wrong database entirely.
supabase/.temp/

# Claude Code local session dir
.claude/

# Scratch scripts run via the alias loader during a session — never shipped.
# (Two were swept in by `git add -A` on 2026-09-09; this stops a repeat.)
*.tmp.mjs
scripts/tmp/
.ui-shots/
